\chapter{Reported hours from Medvind, the \texttt{medvind} module}%
\label{medvind}

TAs report the hours they have worked in Medvind, Visma's time-reporting
system, and the course responsible attests them there.
We book TAs in nytid (\cref{cli.hr}), so nytid knows what each TA
\emph{should} have reported.
Comparing the two by hand means scrolling the attest view in a web
browser and holding the booking sheet in one's head.
This module is the first half of automating that comparison: it reads
the reported times out of Medvind, so that a script---or a later nytid
command---can set them against the bookings.

Medvind has no public API.
Its web interface, however, is a JavaScript application that fetches
everything it shows as JSON from a single endpoint, and once we hold
the same cookies as the browser we can call that endpoint ourselves.
Everything we know about it comes from a recording of the browser's
traffic (a HAR file) while the attest view was open.
The module therefore has three parts, which we present in the order a
request experiences them:
\begin{description}
\item[Logging in (\cref{medvind.login})] Medvind sits behind KTH's
  single sign-on with multi-factor authentication.
  The [[weblogin]] package handles the password and the MFA steps; we
  add the one handler it lacks, [[MedvindSSO]], which forwards the
  SAML and WS-Federation relay pages between the systems.
\item[The data API (\cref{medvind.api})] The class [[Medvind]] wraps
  the JSON endpoint: one method per kind of request, no decisions.
\item[Records to rows (\cref{medvind.rows})] Pure functions turn the
  records Medvind returns into the rows we print, and select the rows
  we are interested in.
  These are the parts a comparison with the bookings will reuse.
\end{description}

<<[[medvind.py]]>>=
