\section{Logging in}%
\label{medvind.login}

The browser's way into the attest view is a chain of five pages.
Opening [[MvWeb.aspx?ws=ATTEST]] redirects to Medvind's own login
service, [[login.medvind.visma.com]], and from there:
\begin{enumerate}
\item an auto-submitting form posts a [[SAMLRequest]] to KTH's identity
  provider, the ADFS server at [[login.ug.kth.se]];
\item ADFS answers with its username and password form;
\item after the password, ADFS shows the Authenticator
  number-matching page, which polls until the user approves on the
  phone;
\item an auto-submitting form posts the [[SAMLResponse]] back to
  Medvind's login service;
\item that service in turn posts a WS-Federation token ([[wresult]])
  to [[kth.medvind.visma.com]], which redirects to the attest view and
  sets the cookie [[medvind.sso.token]].
\end{enumerate}
The last cookie is the one that matters: the data API starts its
session from it (\cref{medvind.api}).

Steps 2 and 3 are already solved.
[[weblogin.kth.UGlogin]] fills in KTH's password form, and
[[weblogin.microsoft.AzureMFA]] recognises the number-matching page by
its [[validEntropyNumber]] element, shows the number and polls.
The relays in steps 1, 4 and 5 are not handled by any [[weblogin]]
handler: they are plain HTML forms that a browser submits with
JavaScript, and [[requests]] does not run JavaScript.
So the one thing this section adds is a handler that submits relay
forms.

\subsection{Recognising a relay page}

A relay page is a form that carries a security token from one system
to the next.
Contrast it with the two other forms the chain shows us: the password
form has a [[Password]] field, and the MFA page's form carries only
ADFS's own [[AuthMethod]] and [[Context]].
The relay forms are the only ones with a [[SAMLRequest]], a
[[SAMLResponse]] or a [[wresult]] field, so the presence of one of
those is what we test for.
<<constants>>=
RELAY_INPUTS = frozenset({"SAMLRequest", "SAMLResponse", "wresult"})
@

The function returns everything needed to submit the form, so that the
handler below does not have to parse the page a second time.
Responses that are not HTML---the JSON from the data API, for
instance, also passes through the session's handlers---are rejected
before we parse them, whatever their body contains.
<<functions>>=
def relay_form(response):
    """Return `(method, url, data)` for an SSO relay form, or `None`.

    A relay form is one with a `SAMLRequest`, `SAMLResponse` or
    `wresult` field; `url` is the absolute address it posts to.
    """
    if "html" not in response.headers.get("Content-Type", ""):
        return None
    try:
        tree = html.fromstring(response.content)
    except (etree.ParserError, ValueError):
        return None
    for form in tree.xpath("//form"):
        data = {
            field.name: field.value or ""
            for field in form.xpath(".//input")
            if field.name
        }
        if RELAY_INPUTS & data.keys():
            url = urllib.parse.urljoin(response.url, form.action or "")
            return (form.method or "POST"), url, data
    return None
