\subsection{Where a token may go}

Recognising a relay form is not enough to submit it.
A [[SAMLResponse]] is the proof that we have logged in; submitting it
wherever a page's form happens to point would hand that proof to
whoever controls the page.
So we relay only to the three hosts the chain uses, and never from
HTTPS to plain HTTP, where anyone on the network could read the token.
<<constants>>=
RELAY_HOSTS = frozenset(
    {
        "login.medvind.visma.com",
        "login.ug.kth.se",
        "kth.medvind.visma.com",
    }
)
<<functions>>=
def safe_relay_target(page_url, target_url, hosts=RELAY_HOSTS):
    """Return True if a token on `page_url` may be posted to `target_url`.

    The target must be one of `hosts` and must not downgrade the
    connection from HTTPS to HTTP.
    """
    page = urllib.parse.urlsplit(page_url)
    target = urllib.parse.urlsplit(target_url)
    downgrade = page.scheme == "https" and target.scheme != "https"
    return target.hostname in hosts and not downgrade
@

This is the security property of the module, so we check each way a
target can fail, with the real hosts:
<<test functions>>=
def test_safe_relay_target():
  medvind = "https://login.medvind.visma.com/MedvindSSO/Login/"
  assert safe_relay_target(medvind, "https://login.ug.kth.se/adfs/ls/")
  assert not safe_relay_target(medvind, "https://attacker.example/")
  assert not safe_relay_target(medvind, "http://login.ug.kth.se/adfs/ls/")
  assert safe_relay_target("http://127.0.0.1:8000/a",
                           "http://127.0.0.1:8000/b", LOCAL)
