\subsection{Assembling the session}

A session that logs into Medvind needs two handlers: [[AzureMFA]] for
step 3 and ours for the rest.
Their order matters, because each response is checked against the
handlers once, in order.
The page that follows the MFA is the relay of step 4, and it is
[[AzureMFA]] that returns it, in the nested check after the password.
With our handler after [[AzureMFA]], that same check goes on to
forward the relay; with our handler first, the check would already be
past it, and the relay would reach the caller unsubmitted.
We restrict [[AzureMFA]] to KTH's identity provider, as [[weblogin]]
recommends, so that no other page can make it poll.

The session also has to say who it is.
Medvind's login service sits behind a firewall that answers
[[requests]]' default [[User-Agent]], [[python-requests/]]\emph{version},
with \enquote{403 Forbidden}, so the chain stops at its first page.
A browser's identification gets through, but so does any other we
tried, [[nytid]] included---the firewall turns away that one generic
client, not everything that is not a browser.
So we need not pretend to be a browser: we name nytid and where it
comes from, which is also what tells Medvind's administrators whom to
ask about the traffic.
<<constants>>=
MFA_URL = "https://login.ug.kth.se"
USER_AGENT = "nytid (https://github.com/dbosk/nytid)"
<<functions>>=
def make_session(username, password, hosts=RELAY_HOSTS, mfa_url=MFA_URL):
    """Return a session that logs into Medvind through KTH's sign-on.

    `username` and `password` are the KTH credentials; `hosts` and
    `mfa_url` say where relays may go and where to expect MFA.
    """
    session = weblogin.AutologinSession(
        [
            weblogin.microsoft.AzureMFA(url=mfa_url),
            MedvindSSO(username, password, hosts=hosts),
        ]
    )
    session.headers["User-Agent"] = USER_AGENT
    return session
<<imports>>=
import weblogin.microsoft
@

The test that matters most replays the whole chain on a session built
by [[make_session]]: a relay leads to a password form, the password to
the MFA page, the MFA poll to a second relay, and the relays to the
final page.
It checks that the password reached the identity provider in the form
KTH expects (with [[@ug.kth.se]] appended by [[UGlogin]]), that the MFA
page was polled, that every token was relayed, and that the caller
receives the final page---which is only possible if the nested checks
and the order of the handlers work as described above.
Every request must also carry nytid's [[User-Agent]], the relays and the
password included.
The password form's action is absolute because [[UGlogin]] resolves a
relative action against [[login.ug.kth.se]], not against the page.
[[AzureMFA]] announces the number through the command in
[[WEBLOGIN_MFA_NOTIFICATION]], which we set to [[true]] so that the
test neither prints nor runs whatever command the user has configured;
it also waits two seconds before polling, which this test pays once.
