\subsection{Sharing the cookies with \texttt{ladok3}}

A session from [[make_session]] works, but on its own it would cost a
password and an MFA approval on every run.
The [[ladok3]] package keeps a shared, logged-in session,
[[ladok3.session.session]], and saves it---cookies included,
encrypted---when the program exits.
Its cookies include the KTH identity provider's whenever [[ladok]] or
[[kthutils]] was used recently, and with ADFS's \enquote{keep me
signed in} cookie, steps 2 and 3 are then skipped altogether.

What we want from that session is its cookies, not its handlers.
So we give our session [[ladok3]]'s cookie jar---the same object, not
a copy---and keep our own handlers.
Contrast the alternative of adding our handler to [[ladok3]]'s
session: the handler would be pickled into [[ladok3]]'s cache with
everything else, take part in the sign-on of every later [[ladok]] and
[[kthutils]] run, and make each of them complain about a handler it
cannot load whenever nytid is not installed beside it.
Sharing the jar avoids all three, and loses nothing: what we add to the
jar---Medvind's cookies, refreshed identity-provider cookies---is saved
with [[ladok3]]'s session at exit, so the next run of either program
benefits.
The credentials are [[ladok3]]'s too: [[ladok login]] stores the KTH
username and password in the system keyring.

We import [[ladok3]] inside the function.
Importing it is not free, and a caller that brings its own session---a
test, for instance---should not pay for it, nor need LADOK credentials.
<<functions>>=
def shared_session():
    """Return a session for Medvind that shares `ladok3`'s cookies.

    The cookies, which `ladok3` saves between runs, spare us the
    password and the MFA approval after a recent login through `ladok`,
    `kthutils` or this module.  Requires `ladok login` to have stored
    the KTH credentials.
    """
    import ladok3
    import ladok3.cli

    _, credentials = ladok3.cli.load_credentials()
    session = make_session(credentials["username"], credentials["password"])
    session.cookies = ladok3.session.session.cookies
    return session
@


\subsection{Keeping the MFA number off standard output}

[[AzureMFA]] shows the number to type into the Authenticator app with
[[figlet]], or with [[print]] when [[figlet]] is missing.
Both write to standard output.
For a command whose standard output is data---[[nytid hr medvind hours]]
prints rows for scripts---that is doubly wrong when the output is
piped: the user never sees the number, so the login cannot complete,
and the number ends up in the data.
The right fix belongs in [[weblogin]] (dbosk/weblogin\#48).
Until it is released we guard the login: for its duration, standard
output is pointed at standard error.

The guard has to work at two levels.
[[print]] writes to [[sys.stdout]], the Python object, which
[[contextlib.redirect_stdout]] swaps out.
[[figlet]] is a separate process that inherits file descriptor~1,
which no Python object controls, so we also duplicate descriptor~2
onto descriptor~1 and restore the original afterwards.
We name the descriptors by number rather than ask [[sys.stdout]] for
its [[fileno()]].
The two need not agree: a program that has replaced [[sys.stdout]]
(a test runner capturing output, for one) answers with the
descriptor of its replacement, while a subprocess still writes to
descriptor~1.
Only when descriptor~1 is not open at all is there nothing to divert
at that level, and the first level is all we do.
<<functions>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def stdout_to_stderr():
    """Send everything written to standard output to standard error.

    Covers both Python's `sys.stdout` and file descriptor 1, so that
    subprocesses are redirected too.
    """
    sys.stdout.flush()
    try:
        saved_fd = os.dup(STDOUT_FD)
    except OSError:
        saved_fd = None
    if saved_fd is not None:
        os.dup2(STDERR_FD, STDOUT_FD)
    try:
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(sys.stderr):
            yield
    finally:
        if saved_fd is not None:
            sys.stderr.flush()
            os.dup2(saved_fd, STDOUT_FD)
            os.close(saved_fd)
