\section{The data API}%
\label{medvind.api}

Every request the attest view makes goes to the same address,
[[MvData.aspx]], with three query parameters: the module and the
function to call, and the output format, which is always JSON.
The parameters of the function travel in the body, as XML:
\begin{quote}
[[<postdata><postParams><PLANID>...</PLANID>...</postParams></postdata>]]
\end{quote}
sent with the content type [[application/json]]---the content type is
wrong, but it is what the browser sends, and we copy the browser.

A Medvind session starts from the single sign-on cookie: the function
[[CREATE]] of the module [[SESSION]] takes the cookie's value and
returns a session token, the user's identity and, among much else, the
organisation units the user last worked with.
Every later request must carry three parameters: the token, the user
and the role.
The attest view is the work of a manager, so the role is
[[MANAGER]]; the same person has an [[EMPLOYEE]] role for reporting
their own hours, which we have no use for here.
<<constants>>=
SSO_COOKIE = "medvind.sso.token"
WORKSPACE = "ATTEST"
ROLE = "MANAGER"
@

\subsection{Encoding the parameters}

The values are user data---dates, but also identifiers that we pass
on from Medvind's own answers---so we escape them as XML text.
We keep the parameter names exactly as given: they are not uniform
([[sso_wif]] is in lower case, [[PLANID]] in upper), and Medvind's
server is the authority on how they are spelled.
<<functions>>=
def postdata(params):
    """Encode `params` as the XML body Medvind's data API expects."""
    fields = "".join(
        f"<{name}>{escape(str(value))}</{name}>"
        for name, value in params.items()
    )
    return f"<postdata><postParams>{fields}</postParams></postdata>"
<<imports>>=
from xml.sax.saxutils import escape
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_postdata_wraps_and_escapes_parameters():
  assert postdata({"PLANID": "P1", "sso_wif": "a<b&c"}) == (
    "<postdata><postParams><PLANID>P1</PLANID>"
    "<sso_wif>a&lt;b&amp;c</sso_wif></postParams></postdata>")
@

\subsection{The \texttt{Medvind} class}

The class holds the session and Medvind's session token, and has one
method per request.
It makes no decisions about what to fetch or how to present it; those
belong to the caller and to the functions of \cref{medvind.rows}.
That separation is also what makes it testable: the class is the only
part of the module that talks to Medvind, so it is the only part that
needs a server in the tests.

When no session is given, the class uses the shared one
(\cref{medvind.login}).
Both the session factory and [[BASE_URL]] are looked up when an object
is created, not when the module is loaded, which is the seam the CLI's
tests use.
<<classes>>=
class Medvind:
    """A connection to Medvind's attest view.

    Call `login` before any other method.  `session` defaults to
    `shared_session()`, which shares `ladok3`'s cookies; `base_url`
    defaults to KTH's Medvind.
    """

    def __init__(self, session=None, base_url=None):
        self.__session = session if session is not None else shared_session()
        self.__base_url = base_url or BASE_URL
        self.__token = None
        self.__userid = None
        self.__plans = []
        self.username = None

    <<[[Medvind]] methods>>
@

\paragraph{Calling a function.}
Medvind reports failures inside the JSON, in [[mvresult.success]],
with HTTP status 200, so checking the status alone would let a failed
call through as an empty answer.
We check both.
<<[[Medvind]] methods>>=
def call(self, module, func, **params):
    """Call `func` of `module` in Medvind's data API; return the JSON.

    Adds the session parameters once `login` has run.  Raises
    `MedvindError` if Medvind reports a failure.
    """
    if self.__token:
        params.update(TOKEN=self.__token, USERID=self.__userid, ROLE=ROLE)
    response = self.__session.post(
        f"{self.__base_url}/MvData.aspx",
        params={"MODULE": module, "OUT": "JSON", "FUNC": func},
        data=postdata(params).encode("utf-8"),
        headers={
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
            "X-Requested-With": "XMLHttpRequest",
        },
    )
    response.raise_for_status()
    result = response.json()
    if not result.get("mvresult", {}).get("success"):
        raise MedvindError(
            f"Medvind {module}/{func} failed: {result.get('mvresult')}"
        )
    return result
<<classes>>=
class MedvindError(Exception):
    """Medvind's data API reported a failure."""
@

\paragraph{Logging in.}
We open the attest view exactly as the browser does and let the
session's handlers follow the sign-on chain.
Afterwards the cookie tells us whether we got through: whatever the
chain ended in, without [[medvind.sso.token]] there is no Medvind
session to start.
The cookie is host-only, so we look it up for Medvind's host; a lookup
by name alone fails when another host in the jar set a cookie with the
same name---and the jar is [[ladok3]]'s, shared with other programs.
<<[[Medvind]] methods>>=
def login(self):
    """Log into Medvind through single sign-on and start a session.

    May ask for MFA approval; the number is shown on standard error.
    Raises `weblogin.AuthenticationError` if the sign-on fails.
    """
    with stdout_to_stderr():
        self.__session.get(
            f"{self.__base_url}/MvWeb.aspx", params={"ws": WORKSPACE}
        )
    host = urllib.parse.urlsplit(self.__base_url).hostname
    sso_token = self.__session.cookies.get(SSO_COOKIE, domain=host)
    if not sso_token:
        raise weblogin.AuthenticationError(
            f"Medvind sign-on did not yield the {SSO_COOKIE} cookie"
        )
    session = self.call("SESSION", "CREATE", sso_wif=sso_token)["session"]
    self.__token = session["token"]
    self.__userid = session["userid"]
    self.__plans = session.get("lastplan", [])
    self.username = session.get("username")
@

\paragraph{Plans.}
Medvind organises people in \emph{plans}, nodes in the organisation
tree, and the attest view shows one plan at a time.
A plan is identified by its [[id]], but requests also need its
[[strpos]], the path from the root of the tree to the node.
The session answer lists the plans the user last worked with, most
recent first, with both; the recorded traffic contains no request that
lists the whole tree, so these are the plans we can offer.
<<[[Medvind]] methods>>=
def plans(self):
    """Return the plans the user last worked with, most recent first.

    Each plan is a dictionary with at least `id`, `text` and `strpos`.
    """
    return list(self.__plans)
@

\paragraph{The reported times.}
The attest view loads the times with [[LOAD_TIMELIST]] of the module
[[APPROVE]], for a plan and a range of dates.
We default to the most recent plan, which is the one the web interface
opens with.
<<[[Medvind]] methods>>=
def timelist(self, start, end, plan=None):
    """Return the reported times from `start` to `end` (dates).

    `plan` is a plan id from `plans`; it defaults to the most recent.
    Each time is a dictionary as Medvind returns it, see `record_to_row`.
    """
    plan = self.__plan(plan)
    result = self.call(
        "APPROVE",
        "LOAD_TIMELIST",
        PLANID=plan["id"],
        STRPOS=plan["strpos"],
        LOADFROM=start.isoformat(),
        LOADUNTIL=end.isoformat(),
        VIEW="PLAN",
    )
    return result.get("timeapprove", [])

def __plan(self, plan_id):
    if not self.__plans:
        raise MedvindError("Medvind returned no plans for this user")
    if plan_id is None:
        return self.__plans[0]
    for plan in self.__plans:
        if plan["id"] == plan_id:
            return plan
    known = ", ".join(plan["id"] for plan in self.__plans)
    raise ValueError(f"unknown plan {plan_id}, known plans: {known}")
@

