\section{From records to rows}%
\label{medvind.rows}

A time, as Medvind returns it, is a dictionary of Swedish
abbreviations.
\Cref{tab:medvind-fields} lists the fields we use.
The rest are internal identifiers ([[persnr]], [[anstnr]],
[[placid]], [[planid]]) and the codes behind the texts.

\begin{table}
  \begin{sidecaption}{The fields of a reported time that we
    use.}[tab:medvind-fields]
    \begin{tabular}{ll}
      \toprule
      \textbf{Field} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
      \midrule
      [[fnamn]], [[enamn]] & first name, last name \\
      [[realpnr]] & personnummer, [[YYMMDD-NNNN]] \\
      [[datum]] & date, [[YYYY-MM-DD]] \\
      [[starttid]], [[sluttid]] & start and end time, [[HH:MM]] \\
      [[rastminut]] & break, in minutes \\
      [[tidtyptext]] & kind of time, \eg [[Student(165)]] \\
      [[tidtyp_orsak]] & the TA's comment, \eg [[DD1301 labbpass]] \\
      [[arbledare_orsak]] & the work leader the TA chose \\
      [[atteststatus]] & [[A]] once attested, otherwise absent \\
      [[avv_konto]] & the account the time is charged to \\
      \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
  \end{sidecaption}
\end{table}

What is \emph{not} there matters as much.
There is no KTH username and no e-mail address, in this answer or in
any other the attest view receives.
A TA is identified by name and personnummer only, which is why the
selection below (\cref{medvind.select}) offers both, and why matching
the times against the bookings will have to go by name or
personnummer too.

\subsection{Hours}

The hours of a time are the time between start and end, less the
break.
An end before the start means the time ran past midnight, which is the
only reading under which such a time is a valid one.

There is one more field that looks relevant, [[dygn]] (day).
In the recorded data it is [[0]] for every time except a pair on the
same date, both from 15:00 to 17:00 and neither attested, one with
[[-1]] and one with [[1]].
Whatever the field means, it does not mean that a time crosses
midnight, and adding a day for it would turn two hours into 26.
We therefore leave it out of the calculation.
<<functions>>=
def hours(record):
    """Return the hours of a reported time: end − start − break."""
    start = minutes_since_midnight(record["starttid"])
    end = minutes_since_midnight(record["sluttid"])
    if end < start:
        end += MINUTES_PER_DAY
    return (end - start - int(record.get("rastminut") or 0)) / 60

def minutes_since_midnight(clock):
    """Return the minutes since midnight of an `HH:MM` time."""
    hour, minute = clock.split(":")
    return int(hour) * 60 + int(minute)
<<constants>>=
MINUTES_PER_DAY = 24 * 60
@

The recorded times have no breaks, so we cover them, and the passing
of midnight, with times of our own:
<<test functions>>=
def test_hours_subtracts_the_break():
  assert hours({"starttid": "08:00", "sluttid": "11:45",
                "rastminut": "0"}) == 3.75
  assert hours({"starttid": "08:00", "sluttid": "17:00",
                "rastminut": "60"}) == 8

def test_hours_across_midnight():
  assert hours({"starttid": "22:00", "sluttid": "01:30"}) == 3.5

def test_hours_ignores_dygn():
  assert hours({"starttid": "15:00", "sluttid": "17:00",
                "dygn": "-1"}) == 2
