\subsection{A row per time}

A row is what we print, so it is made for reading and for scripts at
the same time.
Two normalisations make that possible.
The comment is free text and may contain line breaks or tabs---the
recording has a comment with [[\r\n]] in it---either of which would
split a tab-separated row; we collapse all white space to single
spaces.
The account is padded to fixed widths in some records
([[JXX     ,110210  ,]]) and not in others ([[JXX,110210,]]); stripping
the padding of each part makes the same account print the same, so
that a script can group by it.

The hours are rounded to two decimals and printed without trailing
zeros, since nearly all reported times are whole quarters of an hour:
\enquote{2.75} rather than \enquote{2.750000}, and \enquote{4} rather
than \enquote{4.0}.
The personnummer is not part of the row.
We need it to select a TA, but printing it would spread it into every
file and terminal a script writes to.
<<constants>>=
ROW_COLUMNS = (
    "name",
    "date",
    "start",
    "end",
    "hours",
    "type",
    "comment",
    "leader",
    "status",
    "account",
)
<<functions>>=
def record_to_row(record):
    """Return a reported time as a tuple of strings, see `ROW_COLUMNS`."""
    return (
        one_line(full_name(record)),
        record.get("datum", ""),
        record.get("starttid", ""),
        record.get("sluttid", ""),
        f"{round(hours(record), 2):g}",
        one_line(record.get("tidtyptext", "")),
        one_line(record.get("tidtyp_orsak", "")),
        one_line(record.get("arbledare_orsak", "")),
        record.get("atteststatus", ""),
        normalise_account(record.get("avv_konto", "")),
    )

def normalise_account(account):
    """Return `account` without the padding around its parts."""
    return ",".join(part.strip() for part in account.split(","))

def full_name(record):
    """Return the full name of the person who reported `record`."""
    return f"{record.get('fnamn', '')} {record.get('enamn', '')}".strip()

def one_line(text):
    """Return `text` with every run of white space as a single space."""
    return " ".join(str(text).split())
@

The recorded times give us both normalisations, and a time with
fields left out must still give a full row:
